+# Tracker Scope integrity
+
+## Context
+
+`pkg/provider/clickup/clickup.go` supplies native List identity only after `FetchItem`. `pkg/provider/vikunja/vikunja.go` currently reads `project_id` but does not copy it to the fetched Work Item, so removing every event fallback would break its configured routing. `provider.TrackerEvent.Item` is already defined as the fallback when a read cannot supply authoritative state.
+
+ADR-0014 is accepted and binds the Work Target to the Work Item. ADR-0015 is proposed and describes a broader provider-opaque routing policy; this repair does not treat it as ratified or implement its new rule table, SPI removal or strict-mode policy. Current source was checked directly rather than relying on the older architecture narrative.
+
+## Goals and non-goals
+
+Restore fetched ClickUp Scope, prevent a contradictory event from authorizing either possible target, and preserve current fallback compatibility. The proposal lists excluded platform and connector work. PV-013 is only partially delivered until its durable audit and connection-scoped routing requirements are completed and its tests are executed.
+
+## Change-local behavior
+
+The reconciliation occurs in `Server.mirror`, before the existing `pinTeam` and `resolveTarget` calls:
+
+| Successful fetched Scope | Event Scope | Result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Present | Empty | Retain fetched Scope |
+| Present | Same value | Retain fetched Scope |
+| Present | Different value | Return fetched identity plus a conflict error |
+| Empty | Present | Retain event Scope for existing adapters |
+| Empty | Empty | Preserve existing unresolved behavior |
+
+A read error continues to use the existing snapshot/event fallback. The tracker handler turns the new conflict error into HTTP 409 and logs both opaque values. It never calls the Store for that conflicting event. This is a consistency guard, not a new work-routing architecture; it does not silently retarget a moved task.
+
+## Risks and trade-offs
+
+- A legitimate task move can produce a stale assignment that now receives HTTP 409. Replaying the same payload will remain conflicting. An operator must confirm the current container and issue a fresh consistent assignment; later polling/reconciliation work can automate this repair.
+- A provider with absent fetched Scope still trusts event Scope for compatibility. In particular, this does not qualify Vikunja current-state scope protection or universal strict routing.
+- Rejection evidence is structured process logging, not a transactionally persisted audit row. Log collection and retention remain deployment concerns; PV-013's durable evidence criterion is still open.
+- Earlier events in a multi-event webhook can already have been accepted before a later event conflicts. Transactional inbox and batch semantics are separate work.
+- Scopes are still provider/container identifiers in the existing configuration model. Multiple source connections with overlapping native IDs require the planned identity contract, not an assumption introduced here.
+
+Claim and queue predicates are unchanged, so the KEDA query needs no corresponding change. A rejected event has no Work Item, Shift or Run mutation; the existing store transaction and sweeper behavior remain responsible for events that are accepted.
+
+## Verification
+
+The tests use the actual ClickUp parser and an `httptest` task endpoint to exercise signed thin payload → fetched List → Team pin → target resolution. Separate cases cover conflict refusal before any database access, matching and missing fetched Scope, and both read-failure fallbacks.
+
+Go was unavailable in the authoring environment. Compilation, formatting, these regressions, proof that they fail against the unfixed behavior, the full Postgres-backed package suite and the repository's CI gates are pending. No Go execution result is claimed.
